layout: base.njk
permalink: /index.html
title: false
---
{% import "card.njk" as cards %}

{# ---------------------------------------------------------------- hero --- #}
{#
   The cinematic title card, .cine-stage — the same block block_test_page_e.html
   demonstrates, with the site's own name set in it. The CSS is a component in
   main.css; nothing about the treatment lives on this page.

   The title is the one part that is built rather than written, because the name
   comes from site_settings.json and every letter needs its own delay. Two
   loops: words, so a multi-word name wraps between words rather than mid-word,
   and letters inside them.

   DELAYS is a fixed table, not a random draw. The values are the ones frozen
   into variant E, and a fixed table is what keeps the page static — the scatter
   is identical on every build and on every visit, so the markup is cacheable
   and the title develops the same way twice.

   The stride of 7 is why the table is not simply read from the start for each
   word. 7 and 15 are coprime, so a word beginning at offset 7·n lands on a
   different run of the table than the word before it; read from index 0 every
   time and a two-word name would develop both words in step, which is the one
   pattern the scatter exists to avoid.

   The letters are aria-hidden and the whole name is on the h1's aria-label: a
   screen reader gets "SITE_NAME", not fifteen separate letters.
#}
{% set DELAYS = [0.32, 1.05, 0.61, 0.88, 0.14, 1.22, 0.47, 0.73,
                 1.36, 0.09, 0.96, 0.52, 1.11, 0.28, 0.67] %}

<section class="cine-stage">
  <div class="cine-media" aria-hidden="true">
    {#- An empty src is not a blank image: the browser re-requests the current
        page and paints the HTML as one. `default_image` defaults to "" in
        settings.js, so a project that has not set one hit exactly that on its
        homepage. Same guard card.njk already carries. -#}
    {%- if settings.default_image %}
    <img src="{{ settings.default_image }}" alt=""{{ settings.default_image | sizeAttrs | safe }}
         loading="eager" decoding="async">
    {%- endif %}
    <div class="cine-scrim"></div>
  </div>
  <div class="cine-grain" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <div class="cine-card">


    {# The site mark, over the name — the two are one lockup, with the eyebrow
       above as its label. svg/logo.svg as a CSS mask, so it is painted with the
       title's own currentColor and needs no light and dark copies; see
       .cine-logo in input.css. Decorative: the h1 below is the site name, and
       this is the same name drawn. #}
    <div class="cine-logo" aria-hidden="true"></div>

    <h1 class="cine-title" aria-label="{{ settings.name }}">
      {%- for word in settings.name.split(" ") -%}
        {%- set stride = loop.index0 * 7 -%}
        <span aria-hidden="true">
        {%- for letter in word | list -%}
          <span class="cine-letter" style="--letter-delay:{{ DELAYS[(stride + loop.index0) % DELAYS.length] }}s">{{ letter }}</span>
        {%- endfor -%}
        </span>
      {%- endfor -%}
    </h1>

    <div class="cine-rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>

    {# No .cine-actions here, deliberately. The title card is the front door and
       nothing else; the nav bar above it already carries Journal and About, so
       a pair of buttons repeating them is a second navigation over the first.
       The scroll cue below is the only thing asking for a click, and
       what it asks for is to keep reading. The component still ships
       .cine-actions and .cine-btn — block_test_page_e.html uses them. #}
    <p class="cine-tagline">{{ settings.description }}</p>
  </div>

  {# The scroll cue — the site's own component, .hero-scroll-cue, which works
     over this stage because .cine-stage declares the three --hero-* tokens it
     reads. The travel is the site's own smooth scrolling, so there is no script
     behind it. The sentinel below is its landing point and belongs to this
     block — keep the two together.

     .hero-scroll-cue-center is the one departure from the way the other stages
     use it. Everywhere else the cue sits in the lower RIGHT corner, which is
     right for a hero whose copy is set left: the mark is out of the type's way
     and the corner is where the eye finishes. This card is centred — logo,
     name, rule and tagline all on one axis — and a corner mark was the only
     thing on the screen off it. It keeps the same bottom line; only the
     horizontal placement changes, and only here. #}
  <a class="hero-scroll-cue hero-scroll-cue-center" href="#hero-end">Scroll to enter <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">&#8595;</span></a>
</section>
<div class="hero-end" id="hero-end"></div>

{# ------------------------------------------------------------- featured --- #}
{% set featured = collections.posts | limit(5) %}
{% if featured.length %}
<section class="shell" style="padding-block:clamp(3rem,8vh,5.5rem);">
  <header style="display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap; align-items:baseline; justify-content:space-between; gap:1rem;">
    <h2 class="display-md">Latest</h2>
    {# The site's own button component, same as the way out at the foot of every
       post — the rule draws in under the label and the arrow steps along on
       hover. The arrow is .btn's own ::after, so it is not typed here.

       .btn-accent-ultra-crimson retunes --btn-accent for this button alone:
       indigo on paper, crimson in the dark. See "Accent modifiers" in
       input.css — every other button on the site keeps the default. #}
    <a class="btn btn-ghost btn-accent-ultra-crimson" href="/blog.html">All entries</a>
  </header>

  <hr class="rule-grad" style="margin-block:1.75rem 2.5rem;">

  <div class="card-grid card-grid--fill">
    {% for post in featured %}{{ cards.card(post, settings) }}{% endfor %}
  </div>
</section>
{% else %}
<section class="shell" style="padding-block:clamp(3rem,8vh,5.5rem);">
  <h2 class="display-md">Latest</h2>
  <hr class="rule-grad" style="margin-block:1.75rem 2.5rem;">
  <p class="lede">
    No published entries yet. Drop a markdown file into <code>input_markdown/</code>
    with <code>draft: false</code> in its front matter and rebuild.
  </p>
</section>
{% endif %}

{# ------------------------------------------------------------- subjects --- #}
{% if collections.tagList.length %}
<section class="shell" style="padding-bottom:clamp(3rem,8vh,5.5rem);">
  <h2 class="display-md">Subjects</h2>
  <hr style="margin-block:1.5rem 0.25rem;">
  {% include "tag_filter.njk" %}
</section>
{% endif %}
